This standalone JavaScript example makes the work count visible:
```javascript
const items = ['Cedar', 'Maple', 'Cedar guide', 'Oak'];
function measure(query) {
  let examined = 0;
  const matches = items.filter(item => {
    examined += 1;
    return item.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase());
  });
  return { matches, examined };
}
console.log(measure('cedar'));
```
The result has matches ['Cedar', 'Cedar guide'] and examined 4. filter visits each item, retaining ones whose callback returns true. toLowerCase makes both texts lowercase for a case-insensitive comparison; includes checks for the query anywhere inside the item.

This complete App needs the React/JSX runner:
```react
function App() {
  const items = React.useMemo(() => ['Cedar', 'Maple', 'Cedar guide'], []);
  const [query, setQuery] = React.useState('cedar');
  const [repeat, setRepeat] = React.useState(0);
  function measure() {
    let examined = 0;
    const matches = items.filter(item => {
      examined += 1;
      return item.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase());
    });
    return { matches, examined };
  }
  const baseline = React.useMemo(measure, [items, query, repeat]);
  const cached = React.useMemo(measure, [items, query]);
  return <main>
    <label>Search <input value={query}
      onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} /></label>
    <button onClick={() => setRepeat(value => value + 1)}>Repeat</button>
    <p>Matches: {cached.matches.length}</p>
    <p>Same count: {baseline.matches.length === cached.matches.length ? 'yes' : 'no'}</p>
  </main>;
}
```
Initially there are two matches. Repeat changes repeat, so the baseline recalculates while cached can reuse its result. Editing query recalculates both. [] gives the list creation no changing dependencies.

The exercise's list uses Array.from({length: 1000}, (_, i) => `Book ${i}`). It creates indexes 0 through 999; _ is a parameter name for an unused value and i is the index. Book 99 matches Book 99 and Book 990 through Book 999: 11 matches. slice(0, 10) displays the first ten without changing the full match array. Check every matching value as well as equal lengths: equal counts alone can hide different results.

```javascript
const matches = ["A", "B", "C"];
console.log(matches.slice(0, 2));
console.log(matches);
```
The arrays are ["A", "B"] and ["A", "B", "C"]. slice receives a start index and an excluded end index, returning a new shallow array without changing matches.

